ening approach in which cervical cells are tested for both HPV and cancer/cell changes. This positions co-testing as a modality choice within the broader cervical screening process rather than a separate, unrelated test. [Data: Entities (6072)]\\n\\nThe relationship from CERVICAL CANCER SCREENING to HPV/PAP CO-TEST indicates that co-testing is a recognized form of screening (i.e., screening can be performed as HPV/Pap co-testing). This reinforces the community structure: a central screening goal links to specific testing configurations applied to collected cervical cells. [Data: Entities (6071, 6072); Relationships (13875)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Gynecologic exam provides the clinical context that includes cervical cancer screening\",\"explanation\":\"GYNECOLOGIC EXAM is described as a clinical examination recommended in this context and explicitly includes screening for cervical cancer and related testing such as Pap and/or HPV testing. This places the gynecologic exam as an umbrella clinical encounter within which cervical screening is performed. [Data: Entities (6070)]\\n\\nThe relationship between GYNECOLOGIC EXAM and CERVICAL CANCER SCREENING formalizes that inclusion: the exam includes screening as a component. As a result, the community’s structure can be read as: an exam context encompasses screening, which then entails specimen collection and test selection. [Data: Entities (6070, 6071); Relationships (13870)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The specimen pathway is reinforced by relationships linking exams, cell samples, and Pap testing\",\"explanation\":\"Beyond the gynecologic-exam-to-screening link, the community includes a specimen-collection pathway showing that during an exam (noted as pelvic exam), a sample of cells may be removed for testing (Pap test). This relationship emphasizes that clinical examination events are directly tied to the creation of a testable cell sample. [Data: Entities (2059); Relationships (3415)]\\n\\nThe PAP TEST-to-CELL SAMPLE relationship fur